.TH LINUX-IP 1 "October 2026" "linux-ip" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linux-ip \- modern Linux networking dashboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linux-ip
[\fB-a\fR|\fB--advanced\fR] [\fB-i\fR|\fB--interactive\fR] [\fB--json\fR] [\fB--no-color\fR] [\fB--ip6\fR] [\fB--public-ip\fR] [\fB--copy\fR \fIFIELD\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows all IP / networking info (interfaces, gateways, routes, DNS, WiFi,
listening ports, firewall) in a modern CLI view. Portable across Ubuntu
and Fedora; never requires root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -a ", " --advanced
Show sysadmin detail view (routes, DNS detail, all interfaces, ports, firewall).
.TP
.BR -i ", " --interactive
Fullscreen live dashboard with click-to-copy and 2s refresh.
Incompatible with --json; requires a terminal.
.TP
.B --json
Machine-readable JSON output.
.TP
.B --no-color
Plain text output. Also honors NO_COLOR.
.TP
.BR --ip6 ", " --ipv6
Include IPv6 addresses, v6 routes, and v6 DNS servers. Default is IPv4 only.
.TP
.B --public-ip
Opt-in public IP lookup via https://api.ipify.org. Off by default.
.TP
.BR --copy " " \fIFIELD\fR
Copy one value to clipboard: ip4|ip6|gateway|dns|public|mac|tailscale.
DNS copies the effective uplink servers, not the 127.0.0.53 stub.
Uses wl-copy, xclip/xsel, or OSC52 fallback.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --version
Print version.
.SH EXAMPLES
linux-ip
.br
linux-ip --advanced
.br
linux-ip --json | jq .interfaces
.br
linux-ip --copy ip4
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ip (8),
.BR ss (8)
